y that hadn't really been  seen in these kinds of movies before  because usually you were getting at neo  a 50/50 split between hero and villain  but here Marvel really put a much  heavier  phasis on villains which resulted in  villains either being sidelined being in  the background not showing up for a lot  of the movie or not even getting  revealed until the very end in some  cases but again I think that was  delivered because they decided look we  got to make sure that people love these  heroes and because that's what we're  going to keep bringing over and over and  over and over again  whereas our villains and most of them  are going to die at the end of the movie  if they're not in love with the villain  not the end of the world  now just because they did it on purpose  doesn't mean it's still a problem it is  but I think it's one that they knew they  were setting up for themselves  DC's villain problem is almost the  opposite I think at a lot of ways they  were trying to chase down those highly  memorable villains of things like the  Tim Burton Batman and the Nolan movies  and to be fair it may have been  something that they were deliberately  trying to do that Marvel wasn't which  was to deliver powerful memorable  villains because honestly Marvel again  and I'm restricting this to the movies  I'm not talking about the Netflix I'm  not talking about the TV shows I'm not  talking about any of that I'm just  talking about the movies just to  streamline this conversation but Marvel  was not filling the villain niche and I  think Deezy thought they could do that  but let's look at the villains they've  actually given us so far  first Manor steel we had General Zod  probably the best villain that the DCU  had delivered so far and a big part of  that is Michael Shannon he is a good  powerful presence he delivered a  slightly over-the-top but a pretty  memorable performance and it worked he  was credible as a threat and he was a  pretty successful villain then we got  BBS first off we had lex luthor by jesse  eisenberg and just know and then  doomsday at the end of it who just was  had no personality who was a big hulking  monster he only showed up at the end  there wasn't a lot to him he was  functional as far as being a boss fight  but that's really all he was Suicide  Squad enchantress oh god that one that  one was painful I know some people might  bring up the Joker but first of all I  would argue that the Joker isn't really  a villain in the story because he's not  an obstacle to anything that's actually  going on in the plot he's a bad guy sure  but so are the heroes so that doesn't  automatically qualify them for villain  status even if we want to count him as a  villain knowing that you know this is a  bill and his character if he shows up  again hope  be the full-on bad guy of something but  even if we take that route and want to  label it as a villain tear little Joker  at best highly divisive credit to them  for trying they tried to do something  new with him but for the most part it  didn't really seem to take and then  there's Wonder Woman which is DC's most  successful film critically way up there  in terms of box office it has done  phenomenally but I would say that its  villain is one of its weaker aspects and  considered as a spoiler warning for  Wonder Woman because some of this stuff  is towards the end of the movie I am  going to have to talk about it now  interestingly Wonder Woman they DC took  almost a more Marvel excess Caprona the  hero and therefore the villain and by  that I mean when they were making a  particularly BVS but also Man of Steel  to certain extent they really seem to be  banking on built-in cultural awareness  of who these heroes were as a way to not  have to spend as much time really  zeroing in on them and to try and spend  more time with the villains or in the  case of Batman V Superman get away with  splitting the time between two heroes  even though it was our introduction to  one